---
/**
 * The one auth affordance on public pages: "Log in", or "Log out" once the
 * reader has a session.
 *
 * Public pages are prerendered — one HTML file served to everyone — so this
 * cannot be decided on the server. Both links are always in the markup and CSS
 * shows exactly one of them, keyed off a `data-signed-in` attribute that
 * `CustomHead.astro` sets on `<html>` before the first paint. Nothing is
 * fetched and nothing swaps in after load, so there is no flash of the wrong
 * button.
 *
 * The attribute comes from a readable, content-free hint cookie
 * (`SIGNED_IN_HINT_COOKIE`). It grants nothing: forge it and you get a header
 * that lies to you, while every private URL still goes through the guard. That
 * is the trade this makes deliberately — a cosmetic lie the reader tells
 * themselves, in exchange for no flash and no per-page request.
 *
 * "Log in" points at `/private/`, not at the SSO endpoint. The guard is what
 * knows where sign-in lives and what to come back to; sending readers straight
 * at `DOCS_SSO_URL` would skip the `state` nonce and the `returnTo` it stores.
 *
 * Rendered only when sign-in is actually configured. The header asks
 * `authConfigured()`, so a deployment with no `DOCS_*` variables set — a demo,
 * a staging site, a fork that has not wired SSO yet — shows no control at all
 * rather than one that answers 404.
 *
 * **Both links opt out of prefetching, and must stay that way.** Starlight
 * enables `prefetchAll`, so Astro prefetches every link on hover — and a
 * prefetch is a real GET with the reader's cookies. That turns both of these
 * into state changes on hover: prefetching "Log out" destroys the session
 * without a click, and prefetching "Log in" runs the entire SSO round trip
 * (guard → the product → `/auth/callback`) and issues a session nobody asked
 * for. Both were measured, not reasoned about; the second is how a reader who
 * clicks "Log out" and leaves the mouse where it is gets silently signed back
 * in, because "Log in" then occupies the same pixel.
 *
 * Both hrefs go through `withBase`. Astro does not apply the configured `base`
 * to a plain `<a href>` in a component — only Starlight does, and only for the
 * sidebar items it builds. So the "Private docs" entry beside this one comes
 * out as `/docs/private/` on a subpath deployment while a raw href here would
 * stay `/private/` and 404, which is precisely how this went unnoticed the
 * first time.
 */
import { withBase } from '../lib/auth/http.mjs';
---

<div class="auth-control print:hidden">
	<a class="auth-in" href={withBase('/private/')} data-astro-prefetch="false">Log in</a>
	<a class="auth-out" href={withBase('/auth/logout')} data-astro-prefetch="false">Log out</a>
</div>

<style>
	.auth-control {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
	}

	.auth-control a {
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-1);
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		text-decoration: none;
		white-space: nowrap;
		padding: 0.25rem 0.5rem;
		border-radius: 0.25rem;
		line-height: 1;
	}

	.auth-control a:hover {
		color: var(--sl-color-white);
		background-color: var(--sl-color-gray-6);
	}

	/* Signed out is the default, because it is the state a reader with no
	 * cookies is in and the state a forged cookie cannot fake into something
	 * more permissive. */
	.auth-out {
		display: none;
	}

	:global(html[data-signed-in]) .auth-in {
		display: none;
	}

	:global(html[data-signed-in]) .auth-out {
		display: inline-block;
	}
</style>
